I didn't realize there weren't any constraints on
the resulting Cholesky factor other than positive diagonals.
I'm still working on my merit badge in matrices and linear
algebra.

Is there an easy way to constrain L so that
L * L' is a correlation matrix?  Basically each row
of L needs to be unit length so that

    (L * L')[i,i] =  L[i] dot L[i]

                  =  1.

I know we could just divide, but we want to do this in such
a way that the transform is bijective so we can easily
compute the change of variables.

How should we build data types into Stan to support
LKJ?  We can now separate the issue from that of specifying
a basic covariance matrix transform.

In the worst case, we'd rount trip through the
factorizations every leapfrog step, but that would be
very very slow as Ben's made clear in previous messages.

It's not that hard to add basic data types to Stan.  All
we need is:

    mapping to C++ type and getters/setters (trivial if matrix-like,
       but we should think about triangular and symmetric matrix
       types)

    unconstrained -> constrained transform & log abs Jacobian det

    constrained -> unconstrained transform

