I agree. The only difference is that I was thinking something along these lines

Case 1:

parameters {
  cov_matrix(N) Sigma;
}
model {
   Sigma ~ wishart();
  // something with Sigma
}

In this situation, we should use a plain Cholesky parameterization behind the scenes without the user knowing it or having to specify it explicitly. The C++ constructor for the CovMatrix would only initialize the L attribute.

In other words, the following more explicit version should be legal, but we shouldn't force the user to do

parameters {
  chol_factor(N) L;
}
transformed parameters {
  cov_matrix(N) Sigma;
  Sigma <- L * L'
}
model {
   Sigma ~ wishart();
  // something with Sigma
}

But if the user wants to do LKJ, then along the lines of what you said

Case 2:

parameters {
  vector CPCs(N * (N - 1));
  vector sds(N);
}
transformed parameters {
  cov_matrix(N) Sigma;
  Sigma <- LKJ_transformation(CPCs, sds);
}
model {
   double eta;
   eta <- 1.0;
   CPCs ~ LKJ_prior(eta);
   for(i in 1:N) {
     sds[i] ~ something();
   }
   // something with Sigma
}

Again, the Cholesky factorization is hidden from the user but integral to the C++.

Ben

