Or Case 3 for an AR1 situation:

parameters {
  double rho(-1,1);
  double variance(0,);
}
transformed parameters {
  cov_matrix(N) Sigma;
  Sigma <- AR1(rho, variance);
}
model {
  rho ~ uniform(-1,1)
  variance ~ something();
   // something with Sigma
}

In the AR1 case, it is usually better *not* to calculate a Cholesky factor because the covariance matrix, its inverse (which is tridiagonal), and its determinant are known in closed form as a function of rho and variance. So, the CovMatrix constructor in C++ might initialize these three things, but not L or L_inverse. But whatever density function is using Sigma doesn't need to care much about about that. It just needs to check whether the desired attributed is initialized before it tries to use it in a calculation.

Ben

