On 4/3/12 4:31 PM, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> On Tuesday, April 3, 2012 4:11:34 PM UTC-4, Marcus Brubaker wrote:
>
>     I actually think a CovMatrix class is a good idea since they are such an integral part of almost any model.
>
>     My main point was that if we want the model code to do the right thing we either a) need to figure out from the
>     model specification which representation to use when parameterizing the model (I think this is going to be hard and
>     error prone for everything but really simple cases) or have the user tell us, either by hinting when defining a
>     cov_matrix parameter, or by requiring to the user to build the covariance matrix themselves in the model (what I had
>     proposed).
>
>
> I agree. The only difference is that I was thinking something along these lines

These 3 examples are in line with how we've been doing
things up to now (up to parameterizations and some syntax
in variable declarations for vectors).

> Case 1:
>
> parameters {
> cov_matrix(N) Sigma;
> }
> model {
> Sigma ~ wishart();
> // something with Sigma
> }
>
> In this situation, we should use a plain Cholesky parameterization behind the scenes without the user knowing it or
> having to specify it explicitly. The C++ constructor for the CovMatrix would only initialize the L attribute.
>

Right -- the key is that we allow a user to
declare a cov_matrix param Sigma and then do
whatever they want with it.  All this transformation
stuff's in the background (which is why it'll be
easy to swap out LKJ for Cholesky).

> In other words, the following more explicit version should be legal, but we shouldn't force the user to do
>
> parameters {
> chol_factor(N) L;
> }
> transformed parameters {
> cov_matrix(N) Sigma;
> Sigma <- L * L'
> }
> model {
> Sigma ~ wishart();
> // something with Sigma
> }

The nice thing here is that they could also
do something with the entries of L for a prior
then use the resulting covariance matrix as
a parameter, as in:

    L ~ some_funky_distro_on_cholesky_factors();
    for (i in 1:I)
        y[i] ~ multi_normal(mu,Sigma);

A funky distro could be designed for L along the
lines of:

    for (n in 1:N)
        for (n2 in 1:n)
            L[n,n2] ~ log_normal(...);

As Ben illustrates in the next case:

> But if the user wants to do LKJ, then along the lines of what you said
>
> Case 2:
>
> parameters {
> vector CPCs(N * (N - 1));
> vector sds(N);
> }
> transformed parameters {
> cov_matrix(N) Sigma;
> Sigma <- LKJ_transformation(CPCs, sds);
> }
> model {
> double eta;
> eta <- 1.0;
> CPCs ~ LKJ_prior(eta);
> for(i in 1:N) {
> sds[i] ~ something();
> }
> // something with Sigma
> }
>
> Again, the Cholesky factorization is hidden from the user but integral to the C++.

This is the key for letting users write models flexibly
without themselves having to worry too much about the
constraints.

- Bob

