Yes, I mentioned d=10 as a rough upper limit just to give a sense of the difficulty level of the problems I am considering.  Of course we will often work with many more than 10 variables but in that case I think we'd have more structure rather than using a generic covariance model.

Regarding scaled-inv-wishart, I agree that it has convenient conditional conjugacy, but the scaling (thus, the redundant parameterization) appeals to me because it allows the correlations to be controlled (i.e., smoothed, i.e., partially pooled) while allowing a reasonable prior distribution for the scale parameters.  Plain old unscaled inverse-Wishart doesn't do that; the degrees of freedom simultaneously determine the precisions for the corrs and the scales.
A
.

