On 4/6/12 11:16 AM, Matt Hoffman wrote:
> Hi folks,
>
> I went ahead and implemented a vectorized version of the dot_product()
> function for arrays (it's in stan/agrad/matrix.hpp). In an informal
> test, it seems to be around 25x faster than the old version, so if
> you're doing anything that can be expressed as a dot product you
> should probably be using the vectorized version.

Awesome, as I said yesterday.  We've known for ages that
unfolding the auto-difs in the vector ops would be a huge
win.  You won't get this much gain for very short vectors,
but otherwise, the speedup should be the same once you've
"paid for" the allocation of the dot-product vari object.

We should also do this for the dot_product implementations for
Eigen vectors. They'll be able to use the same vari class
the way Matt implemented it.

Then, those dot product vari can be used as the values in
matrix multiplication, which will be speeded up by an equivalent
amount.

> Also, I was unsatisfied with the domain errors that I was getting when
> trying to test moderately large (i.e., O(100)) values for predictors
> in logistic regressions, so I implemented bernoulli_logit_log(), which
> is equivalent to bernoulli_log(inv_logit(theta)) except that it can
> handle large values of theta (e.g., abs(theta)>  20) correctly.

Nice.  We'd been talking about doing just that
while you (Matt) were gone.

http://lingpipe-blog.com/2012/02/16/howprevent-overflow-underflow-logistic-regression/

I'm still not exactly sure
on how to best handle the final arithmetic ops,
so there are a couple of questions inlined.

We need to deal with extreme values of the predictor
and (I'm pretty sure) keep sensible gradient
information.  Matt's computed log bernoulli(n|theta) with:

     T_prob ntheta = (2*n-1) * theta;
     // Handle extreme values gracefully using Taylor approximations.
     const static double cutoff = 20.0;
     if (ntheta > cutoff)
       return -exp(-ntheta);
     else if (ntheta < -cutoff)
       return ntheta;
     else
       return -log(1 + exp(-ntheta));

I am handling a similar case for the simplex transform
using a new function I added, log1p_exp():

  log1p_exp(x) = log(1 + exp(x)) = log_sum_exp(0,x)

Here's how it's defined:

  inline double log1p_exp(const double& a) {
       // like log_sum_exp below with b=0.0
       if (a > 0.0)
         return a + log1p(exp(-a));
       return log1p(exp(a));
   }

Looking at Matt's code, I'm thinking this'll be better
as:

inline double log1p_exp(const double& a) {
       // like log_sum_exp below with b=0.0
       if (a > 20.0)
          return a;
       if (a > 0.0)
         return a + log1p(exp(-a));
       return log1p(exp(a));
   }

What I'm wondering about is the wisdom of adding something
like:

      if (a < -20) return 0;

That'll be OK if we continue to handle the derivative the
usual way.

> So, when implementing a logistic regression in Stan, I recommend
