> A better way to deal with missing data is to
> split the data matrix into two:
>
>  data_obs[...]
>  data_miss[...]

The above might be better sometimes.  But if the data
are an array and there are holes,
which I do not care (at least for the time being in
vol1/bones example), it is a bit difficult to handle by spiting them
(Maybe I am wrong, but I could not think of better one).

>
> The definition of if_else is very simple and it's
> hard to see where it could go wrong:
>
>    template <typename B, typename T>
>    T if_else(B c, T y_true, T y_false) {
>      return c ? y_true : y_false;
>    }
>
> So if_else(c,y_true,y_false) should evaluate
> to y_true if c != 0 and to y_false if c == 0.
> So if data[i] == -1, then data[i] + 1 should be
> 0.  I'm pretty sure the floating point shouldn't
> be going wrong.
>
> The only thing I could think to do here is
> instrument if_else and have it print out c
> (then remove the instrumentation!).

Thanks.  I looked at them again and realized that
what I was doing is like calling
if_else(0, 5.0 / 0, 0).
I was thinking that 5.0 / 0 would not be evaluated
here as in if...else... statement.
I added another term in vol1/bones example,
which is a bit ugly but make it working now.

Now I see both y_true and y_false are passed (and evaluated
sometimes), which is not efficient as only one is
needed, but might not be avoidable.

--
Jiqiang

