On 4/25/12 12:26 PM, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> On Wednesday, April 25, 2012 11:42:23 AM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
 >
>      > We could have
>      > sym_pos_def_matrix but that is a bit long-winded. I'd be happy to
>     stick
>      > with cov_matrix in the .stan file if we had a cov_matrix class in
>     the
>      > C++ file that could represent a covariance matrix in a myriad of
>      > different ways. Then we could parameterize it via the Cholesky
>     factor of
>      > the precision matrix without the user really knowing.
>
>     I agree that having something like a covariance matrix
>     to cache factorizations makes sense. I'm still not sure
>     how to do it, though in terms of when things like Cholesky
>     factors get computed.
>
>
> I would say the rule should be: If a function needs some representation
> of the covariance matrix that is not already present, that function
> should calculate and cache. That way, we just have to check whether
> something has been initialized before we use it.

That makes sense.

>     If we allow general A[m,n] = x;
>     assignments, then we can't guarantee positive definiteness.
>
>
> It's very difficult to allow such assignments in a covariance matrix. We
> can constrain some product-moment correlations to be zero by
> constraining the corresponding CPC appropriately, but that isn't
> implemented yet. Nor do we utilize any sparse matrix operations from
> eigen. What sort of A[m,n] = x assignments are you contemplating besides
> x being a block of zeros?

Nothing in particular.  I'm just imagining someone
doing something like rescaling one entry at a time.

Or just having the covariance matrix be on the left-hand
side of an assignment.

This is all related to whether we only allow operations
that maintain positive definiteness.

>      > I worked out that the LKJ and the onion are particular
>     hyperspherical
>      > transforms.
>
>     Interesting. I have to go into Eigen and figure out
>     what all the pull() and other ops you used were so I
>     can reverse engineer the transform!
>
>
> All that pull stuff in transform.hpp basically just reflects that fact
> that we get "K choose 2" CPCs in an Array and need to stuff them into
> the lower triangle of a Cholesky factor. As in,
>
> L =
> 1
> a x
> b e x
> c f h x
> d g i j x
>
> where x is the square root of the sum-of-squares in its row.

Nice.   The hyperspherical transform is really easy if the
x values here (really 4 different ones) are positive.

Am I right in thinking we could also use a basis of the
form:

L =
0
a 0
b e 0
c f h 0
d g i j 0

where each of the row vectors is of length 1 (i.e., on
the hypersphere)?  You still get 1s on the diagonal, a positive
definite result, and all values constrained between -1 and 1.
I'm just not sure it's an onto mapping given the way you did it.

> So, first
> we pull a:d from the CPCs, and put them in L. Then remark the spot in
> CPCs that we pull from, pull e:g, and put them in L, etc. And calculate
> the diagonal values along the way.

I figured it was something like that.

A more efficient way to do this for stan::agrad::var
(as things stand now) would be to use our own dot_product
ops to produce the result so the gradients are more efficient.
I'm going to roll that into the covariance matrix case
now that it's unit tested.

> The way CPCs work, the i'th row of L turns out to be a (nonuniform) draw
> on the surface of a hypersphere with i dimensions. If there were another
> distribution based on the hyperspheres-to-correlations transformation,
> it would probably be best to make the columns of L.transpose() be the
> hyperspheres.

Thanks -- I didn't realize the connection to CPCs.

>
>      > It is just that they are the only hyperspherical transforms
>      > that imply a known joint density for the correlation matrix. You
>     could
>      > use another density on the hypersphere(s) and get some correlation
>      > matrix, but its joint density would just be (proportional to) the
>      > product of the (independent) hypersphere densities times a Jacobian
>      > determinant.
>
>      > Hard to see what would be the use of implementing that in
>      > stan until someone does it in such a way that the joint density
>     becomes
>      > analytically attractive.
>
>     I'm just thinking about supporting the corr_matrix data type
>     most efficiently in terms of inverse transforms and their
>     Jacobians. The transform only gets applied for initial values.
>
>
> We already have one function for transforming initial values in
> transform.hpp called factor_cov_matrix(). It could be based on that.

Isn't that what's already going on now?

- Bob

